[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Batching, Draw Calls, and GPU Optimization in Unity"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Understanding how Unity communicates with the GPU is vital for achieving high and stable framerates. At the core of rendering performance are draw calls and batching techniques."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"What is a Draw Call?"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A draw call is a command issued by the CPU to the graphics API (such as DirectX or Vulkan) to draw a mesh using a specific material and shader. Preparing these commands creates significant CPU overhead. If you have thousands of objects with unique materials, your CPU will bottleneck long before your GPU does."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"GPU Optimization Strategies: Batching"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"To reduce CPU overhead, Unity combines the geometry of multiple objects into single batches before sending them to the GPU. There are several types of batching available in Unity:"},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Static Batching: Combines static objects into large combined meshes at build time or runtime, reducing draw calls significantly."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"GPU Instancing: Renders identical meshes with varying properties (like color or position) using a single draw call by leveraging hardware instancing."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Dynamic Batching: Automatically batches small moving meshes on the CPU, though it has high CPU overhead and is rarely recommended for modern complex shaders."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing GPU Instancing in Unity"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"To use GPU Instancing, your shader must support it, and the material must have the 'Enable Instancing' checkbox checked. You can also instantiate properties via script using MaterialPropertyBlock."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"using UnityEngine;

public class InstancedSpawner : MonoBehaviour
{
    public Mesh mesh;
    public Material material;
    public int count = 500;

    private Matrix4x4[] matrices;
    private MaterialPropertyBlock propertyBlock;

    void Start()
    {
        matrices = new Matrix4x4[count];
        propertyBlock = new MaterialPropertyBlock();

        for (int i = 0; i < count; i++)
        {
            Vector3 pos = Random.insideUnitSphere * 50f;
            matrices[i] = Matrix4x4.TRS(pos, Quaternion.identity, Vector3.one);
        }
    }

    void Update()
    {
        Graphics.DrawMeshInstanced(mesh, 0, material, matrices, count, propertyBlock);
    }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Monitoring"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Always use the Unity Profiler and the Frame Debugger to inspect your batches. Look at the Rendering statistics panel to monitor 'Batches Saved' and identify if your bottleneck is CPU-bound draw calls or GPU fill-rate limitations."}]
